Drivers seat — **premise verification only. Not a claim, no label changed, grade untouched.**

Triage's 2026-08-09 hold carried an explicit caveat: the pin *"lives in PR #7089's test suite (in flight at filing time), so it was not re-verified against `origin/main`"*. #7089 has since merged, so that is now checkable, and I checked it.

**The pin landed and it is live** — `packages/drivers/driver-turso/src/turso-remote-autonumber-refusal.test.ts:311`:

```ts
it('[known residue, not fixed] an id-bearing upsert that INSERTS still writes NULL', async () => {
  …
  const inserted = await driver.upsert('crm_case', { id: 'never-seen', organization_id: 'orgA', title: 'inserted by upsert' });
  expect(inserted.case_number).toBeNull();
});
```

Not `skip`ped, not a comment, not vacuous — a real assertion, and deliberately **bidirectional**: its own docstring says it is *"stated as an assertion rather than a comment so it cannot quietly become untrue in either direction."* If anyone closes this leg, that test goes red and forces the conversation rather than letting the change land unremarked. Triage's stated reason for holding — "pinned, so it cannot drift silently" — is therefore sound on measured ground, not on an assumption.

Status of the three restart conditions, as measured today:
- **(a)** appetite door on #6944 disposition A — **not opened**. Unchanged.
- **(b)** a first real report of a caller hitting the silent-NULL leg — **none found**.
- **(c)** rework of the upsert-merge path in the #5495/#6943/#7011 family touching this leg — **none**; the pin above still holds.

**Why this seat is not dispatching it, despite the mandate to.** The maintainer's 2026-08-10 direction (「drivers 任务全部可派,v17 只是优先」) removed triage `hold` grades as a *dispatch blocker*, and this seat has acted on that — #6522 ran under exactly that authorization. But it does not convert this card into execution work. Its own body declines to presuppose a conclusion, and its three options are an appetite trade-off rather than three ways to write the same fix:

- **accept the status quo** — no code changes at all;
- **pay a round trip** — buys the leg at the direct cost of the zero-round-trip property #6944's refusal gate was built to have (#7089 asserts zero `execute` calls on a refusal);
- **wait for disposition A** — and the leg disappears on its own, taking the card with it.

Dispatching would mean this seat picking one, which is the maintainer's call, not a PM's and not a dev's. So it stays held — now on a verified premise instead of an unverified one — and what it actually needs is a ruling on #6944's disposition A, not a developer.

